<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Italian Libya</h1><p class="st-meta">1912 CE – 1943 CE · capital Tripoli</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Italy's North African colony, conquered from the Ottomans and subdued by a brutal war, which Mussolini made a settler province before its loss in 1943.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> invaded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tripolitania-vilayet">Tripolitania Vilayet</a> in 1911 and, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ouchy">Treaty of Ouchy</a> in 1912, obtained <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tripolitania">Tripolitania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Resistance confined Italian control to the coast, especially during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, when the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/senussi-order">Senussi Order</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tripolitanian-republic">Tripolitanian Republic</a> held the interior.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From 1922 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a> reconquered the interior. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rodolfo-graziani">Rodolfo Graziani</a> deported much of the nomadic population into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a> where tens of thousands died, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omar-al-mukhtar">Omar al-Mukhtar</a> was hanged in 1931. The two colonies were united as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> in 1934. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italo-balbo">Italo Balbo</a> built the coastal road and settled some 20,000 Italian farmers in 1938.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations">British Commonwealth</a> forces captured <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">Tripoli</a> in January 1943, ending Italian rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> renounced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> in 1947, and the country came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-military-administration-of-libya">British Military Administration of Libya</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/fezzan-ghadames-military-territory">Fezzan-Ghadames Military Territory</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/italian-libya.jpg" alt="Italian Libya" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: F l a n k e r · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Italy_(1861-1946)_crowned.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1912 CE – 1943 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tripoli</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Italo Balbo, Rodolfo Graziani</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (majority); Catholic Christianity (settlers)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Italian, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Italian Tripolitania and Cyrenaica, Libia italiana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1928&amp;focus=italian-libya" aria-label="Italian Libya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5018 983 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Italian Libya lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5338 1239L5338 1239M5338 1239L5638 1347M5338 1239L5334 1251M5338 1239L5304 942M5338 1239L6173 1949M5338 1239L5380 1442M5338 1239L5338 1239"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5338 1239h0M5638 1347h0M5334 1251h0M5304 942h0M6173 1949h0M5380 1442h0M5338 1239h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5338 1239h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5338 1239h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tripoli. <a href="/atlas?year=1928&amp;focus=italian-libya">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
